Lab 20: Final Project - Production ODF Deployment with Multi-Zone Resilience, Automated Backups, and Monitoring
Objectives
By the end of this lab, students will be able to:

Deploy an OpenShift Data Foundation (ODF) StorageCluster configured for multi-zone resilience and verify that storage pods are actually distributed across availability zones
Automate storage provisioning and scheduled volume snapshot backups using Ansible playbooks against an OpenShift cluster
Configure custom Prometheus alerting rules for ODF/Ceph health, capacity, and OSD status
Validate high availability by simulating node/zone disruption and confirming Ceph cluster health is maintained
Perform a disaster recovery drill using volume snapshots, including restore verification and RPO/RTO measurement
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, students should have:

Basic understanding of Kubernetes and OpenShift concepts (Pods, Nodes, Namespaces)
Familiarity with storage concepts (PVs, PVCs, StorageClasses, VolumeSnapshots)
Experience reading and writing YAML configuration files
Basic knowledge of Ansible playbook structure
Access to an OpenShift cluster (version 4.12+) with at least 3 worker nodes spread across 3 availability zones, and cluster-admin privileges
Required Tools
Install the following on your lab workstation before starting (adjust package manager as needed for your OS):

# Verify the OpenShift CLI is installed
oc version --client

# Install Ansible and the required collections
pip install --user ansible
ansible-galaxy collection install kubernetes.core
ansible-galaxy collection install community.general

# Verify jq is available for JSON parsing
jq --version
Log in to your cluster before proceeding:

oc login --token=<your-token> --server=<your-cluster-api-url>
