Task 1: Deploy ODF with Verified Multi-Zone Resilience
Step 1.1: Verify Cluster Zone Topology
Confirm your worker nodes are spread across at least three zones before deploying storage.

# Check cluster nodes and their zone labels
oc get nodes -L topology.kubernetes.io/zone -L node-role.kubernetes.io/worker

# Count distinct zones among worker nodes
oc get nodes -l node-role.kubernetes.io/worker -o jsonpath='{.items[*].metadata.labels.topology\.kubernetes\.io/zone}' | tr ' ' '\n' | sort -u
Expected output should list at least three distinct zone names (for example us-east-1a, us-east-1b, us-east-1c). If fewer than three zones are present, note this limitation and continue with the zones available; the deployment steps still apply.

Step 1.2: Install the ODF Operator
# Create the storage namespace
oc create namespace openshift-storage

# Label the namespace for cluster monitoring integration
oc label namespace openshift-storage openshift.io/cluster-monitoring=true
Create the operator subscription:

cat << 'EOF' > odf-operator-subscription.yaml
apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1
kind: OperatorGroup
metadata:
  name: openshift-storage-operatorgroup
  namespace: openshift-storage
spec:
  targetNamespaces:
  - openshift-storage
---
apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1alpha1
kind: Subscription
metadata:
  name: odf-operator
  namespace: openshift-storage
spec:
  channel: stable-4.14
  installPlanApproval: Automatic
  name: odf-operator
  source: redhat-operators
  sourceNamespace: openshift-marketplace
EOF
oc apply -f odf-operator-subscription.yaml
Wait for the operator to become ready:

oc get csv -n openshift-storage -w
Deliverable: run oc get csv -n openshift-storage. The ODF ClusterServiceVersion should show PHASE: Succeeded before proceeding.

Step 1.3: Label Nodes and Deploy a Multi-Zone StorageCluster
# Get worker nodes and label them for storage placement
WORKER_NODES=$(oc get nodes --no-headers -l node-role.kubernetes.io/worker | awk '{print $1}')

for node in $WORKER_NODES; do
  oc label node "$node" cluster.ocs.openshift.io/openshift-storage="" --overwrite
done
Create the StorageCluster with pod anti-affinity across zones:

cat << 'EOF' > storage-cluster-multizone.yaml
apiVersion: ocs.openshift.io/v1
kind: StorageCluster
metadata:
  name: ocs-storagecluster
  namespace: openshift-storage
spec:
  manageNodes: false
  resources:
    mds:
      limits:
        cpu: "3"
        memory: "8Gi"
      requests:
        cpu: "1"
        memory: "8Gi"
    mon:
      limits:
        cpu: "1"
        memory: "2Gi"
      requests:
        cpu: "1"
        memory: "2Gi"
  storageDeviceSets:
  - name: ocs-deviceset
    count: 1
    replica: 3
    portable: true
    dataPVCTemplate:
      spec:
        accessModes:
        - ReadWriteOnce
        resources:
          requests:
            storage: "100Gi"
        storageClassName: gp3-csi
        volumeMode: Block
    placement:
      nodeAffinity:
        requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
          nodeSelectorTerms:
          - matchExpressions:
            - key: cluster.ocs.openshift.io/openshift-storage
              operator: Exists
      podAntiAffinity:
        preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
        - weight: 100
          podAffinityTerm:
            labelSelector:
              matchExpressions:
              - key: app
                operator: In
                values:
                - rook-ceph-osd
            topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone
    resources:
      limits:
        cpu: "2"
        memory: "5Gi"
      requests:
        cpu: "1"
        memory: "5Gi"
EOF
oc apply -f storage-cluster-multizone.yaml
Monitor rollout:

oc get pods -n openshift-storage -w
Step 1.4: Verify Zone Distribution
This is the key resilience check: confirm that OSD pods actually landed on nodes in different zones, not just that anti-affinity rules were configured.

# List rook-ceph-osd pods with the node they landed on
oc get pods -n openshift-storage -l app=rook-ceph-osd \
  -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,NODE:.spec.nodeName,STATUS:.status.phase

# Cross-reference each node with its zone label
for pod in $(oc get pods -n openshift-storage -l app=rook-ceph-osd -o jsonpath='{.items[*].metadata.name}'); do
  node=$(oc get pod "$pod" -n openshift-storage -o jsonpath='{.spec.nodeName}')
  zone=$(oc get node "$node" -o jsonpath='{.metadata.labels.topology\.kubernetes\.io/zone}')
  echo "$pod  node=$node  zone=$zone"
done
Expected output (values will vary by cluster):

rook-ceph-osd-0-abcde  node=worker-0  zone=us-east-1a
rook-ceph-osd-1-fghij  node=worker-1  zone=us-east-1b
rook-ceph-osd-2-klmno  node=worker-2  zone=us-east-1c
Deliverable: capture this output and confirm at least two distinct zones appear across the OSD pods (three if your cluster has three zones available). If all OSD pods landed on the same zone, the anti-affinity rule is not being honored and the StorageCluster placement spec must be reviewed.

Finally, verify Ceph reports a healthy cluster:

oc rsh -n openshift-storage deployment/rook-ceph-tools ceph status
Expected result: health: HEALTH_OK (or HEALTH_WARN during initial sync, which should clear within a few minutes).

Task 2: Automate Storage Provisioning and Snapshot Backups with Ansible
Step 2.1: Set Up the Ansible Project
mkdir -p ~/odf-automation/{playbooks,inventory,vars}
cd ~/odf-automation

cat << 'EOF' > ansible.cfg
[defaults]
inventory = inventory/hosts
host_key_checking = False
stdout_callback = yaml
EOF

cat << 'EOF' > inventory/hosts
[openshift]
localhost ansible_connection=local

[openshift:vars]
ansible_python_interpreter=/usr/bin/python3
EOF
Step 2.2: Define Storage Variables
cat << 'EOF' > vars/storage-config.yml
---
odf_namespace: openshift-storage

applications:
  - name: database-app
    namespace: odf-demo
    storage_class: ocs-storagecluster-ceph-rbd
    size: 10Gi
    access_mode: ReadWriteOnce
  - name: shared-storage
    namespace: odf-demo
    storage_class: ocs-storagecluster-cephfs
    size: 10Gi
    access_mode: ReadWriteMany

backup_schedule: "0 2 * * *"
EOF
Step 2.3: Create the Storage Provisioning Playbook
cat << 'EOF' > playbooks/provision-storage.yml
---
- name: Provision ODF Storage Resources
  hosts: openshift
  gather_facts: false
  vars_files:
    - ../vars/storage-config.yml

  tasks:
    - name: Create application namespace
      kubernetes.core.k8s:
        name: odf-demo
        api_version: v1
        kind: Namespace
        state: present

    - name: Create PVCs for applications
      kubernetes.core.k8s:
        definition:
          apiVersion: v1
          kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
          metadata:
            name: "{{ item.name }}-pvc"
            namespace: "{{ item.namespace }}"
          spec:
            accessModes:
              - "{{ item.access_mode }}"
            resources:
              requests:
                storage: "{{ item.size }}"
            storageClassName: "{{ item.storage_class }}"
        state: present
      loop: "{{ applications }}"
      loop_control:
        label: "{{ item.name }}"

    - name: Check PVC status
      kubernetes.core.k8s_info:
        api_version: v1
        kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
        name: "{{ item.name }}-pvc"
        namespace: "{{ item.namespace }}"
      register: pvc_status
      loop: "{{ applications }}"
      loop_control:
        label: "{{ item.name }}"

    - name: Display PVC status
      debug:
        msg: "PVC {{ item.item.name }}-pvc is {{ item.resources[0].status.phase }}"
      loop: "{{ pvc_status.results }}"
      loop_control:
        label: "{{ item.item.name }}"
EOF
Run it:

cd ~/odf-automation
ansible-playbook playbooks/provision-storage.yml -v
oc get pvc -n odf-demo
Deliverable: oc get pvc -n odf-demo shows both database-app-pvc and shared-storage-pvc with STATUS: Bound.

Step 2.4: Create the Snapshot Backup Playbook
cat << 'EOF' > playbooks/backup-storage.yml
---
- name: Configure ODF Volume Snapshot Backups
  hosts: openshift
  gather_facts: false
  vars_files:
    - ../vars/storage-config.yml

  tasks:
    - name: Create VolumeSnapshotClass for RBD
      kubernetes.core.k8s:
        definition:
          apiVersion: snapshot.storage.k8s.io/v1
          kind: VolumeSnapshotClass
          metadata:
            name: ocs-storagecluster-rbdplugin-snapclass
          driver: openshift-storage.rbd.csi.ceph.com
          deletionPolicy: Delete
        state: present

    - name: Create a VolumeSnapshot for each application PVC
      kubernetes.core.k8s:
        definition:
          apiVersion: snapshot.storage.k8s.io/v1
          kind: VolumeSnapshot
          metadata:
            name: "{{ item.name }}-snapshot-manual"
            namespace: "{{ item.namespace }}"
          spec:
            volumeSnapshotClassName: ocs-storagecluster-rbdplugin-snapclass
            source:
              persistentVolumeClaimName: "{{ item.name }}-pvc"
        state: present
      loop: "{{ applications }}"
      loop_control:
        label: "{{ item.name }}"

    - name: Wait for snapshots to become ready
      kubernetes.core.k8s_info:
        api_version: snapshot.storage.k8s.io/v1
        kind: VolumeSnapshot
        name: "{{ item.name }}-snapshot-manual"
        namespace: "{{ item.namespace }}"
      register: snap_status
      until: snap_status.resources[0].status.readyToUse | default(false)
      retries: 12
      delay: 10
      loop: "{{ applications }}"
      loop_control:
        label: "{{ item.name }}"
EOF
Run it:

ansible-playbook playbooks/backup-storage.yml -v
oc get volumesnapshot -n odf-demo
Deliverable: oc get volumesnapshot -n odf-demo shows both snapshots with READYTOUSE: true.

Task 3: Configure Custom Monitoring and Alerting for ODF
Step 3.1: Create Custom PrometheusRule for Ceph and PVC Health
cat << 'EOF' > odf-alerts.yaml
apiVersion: monitoring.coreos.com/v1
kind: PrometheusRule
metadata:
  name: odf-storage-alerts
  namespace: openshift-storage
  labels:
    prometheus: k8s
    role: alert-rules
spec:
  groups:
  - name: odf-storage.rules
    rules:
    - alert: CephClusterErrorState
      expr: ceph_health_status == 2
      for: 5m
      labels:
        severity: critical
      annotations:
        summary: "Ceph cluster is in error state"
        description: "Ceph cluster has reported HEALTH_ERR for more than 5 minutes"

    - alert: CephOSDDown
      expr: ceph_osd_up == 0
      for: 5m
      labels:
        severity: warning
      annotations:
        summary: "A Ceph OSD is down"
        description: "OSD {{ $labels.ceph_daemon }} has been down for more than 5 minutes"

    - alert: PersistentVolumeUsageHigh
      expr: (kubelet_volume_stats_used_bytes / kubelet_volume_stats_capacity_bytes) * 100 > 85
      for: 5m
      labels:
        severity: warning
      annotations:
        summary: "Persistent Volume usage is high"
        description: "PVC {{ $labels.persistentvolumeclaim }} in namespace {{ $labels.namespace }} is {{ $value }}% full"
EOF
oc apply -f odf-alerts.yaml
Step 3.2: Verify Alert Rules Are Loaded
oc get prometheusrule odf-storage-alerts -n openshift-storage

# Query the Prometheus API (via the route) to confirm the rules registered
TOKEN=$(oc create token prometheus-k8s -n openshift-monitoring)
PROM_ROUTE=$(oc get route prometheus-k8s -n openshift-monitoring -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')
curl -s -k -H "Authorization: Bearer ${TOKEN}" \
  "https://${PROM_ROUTE}/api/v1/rules" | jq '.data.groups[] | select(.name=="odf-storage.rules") | .rules[].name'
Deliverable: the command prints "CephClusterErrorState", "CephOSDDown", and "PersistentVolumeUsageHigh", confirming Prometheus has loaded the custom rules.

Task 4: Validate High Availability
Step 4.1: Confirm Baseline Cluster Health
oc rsh -n openshift-storage deployment/rook-ceph-tools ceph status
oc rsh -n openshift-storage deployment/rook-ceph-tools ceph osd tree
Expected output: health: HEALTH_OK, with all OSDs listed as up and in.

Step 4.2: Simulate a Zone/Node Failure
Cordon and drain one storage node to simulate loss of an entire zone's compute capacity:

# Pick one storage node to simulate failure
FAIL_NODE=$(oc get nodes -l cluster.ocs.openshift.io/openshift-storage -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
echo "Simulating failure on: $FAIL_NODE"

oc adm cordon "$FAIL_NODE"
oc adm drain "$FAIL_NODE" --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data --force --timeout=120s
Step 4.3: Verify Ceph Remains Available During the Outage
# Ceph should degrade gracefully but remain operational with replica=3
oc rsh -n openshift-storage deployment/rook-ceph-tools ceph status
Expected result: health: HEALTH_WARN (degraded redundancy is expected with one node down), but the cluster still reports it is servicing I/O and no data is unavailable. Confirm this explicitly:

oc rsh -n openshift-storage deployment/rook-ceph-tools ceph status | grep -E "health:|pgs:"
Look for the absence of unavailable or down PG states in the output; degraded PGs are expected and acceptable.

Step 4.4: Restore the Node
oc adm uncordon "$FAIL_NODE"

# Wait for Ceph to recover to HEALTH_OK
oc rsh -n openshift-storage deployment/rook-ceph-tools ceph status
Deliverable: capture the ceph status output before the drain (HEALTH_OK), during the drain (HEALTH_WARN with no unavailable data), and after uncordon (back to HEALTH_OK). This three-state comparison is your HA validation evidence.

Task 5: Disaster Recovery Drill Using Volume Snapshots
Step 5.1: Simulate Data Loss
Delete the original PVC-backed data by removing the database-app PVC (the snapshot created in Task 2 remains intact and independent of the source PVC's lifecycle).

# Record the current PVC for reference, then delete it to simulate loss
oc get pvc database-app-pvc -n odf-demo -o yaml > database-app-pvc-original.yaml
oc delete pvc database-app-pvc -n odf-demo
Step 5.2: Restore from Snapshot (RTO Measurement)
# Record start time for RTO measurement
START_TIME=$(date +%s)

cat << 'EOF' > restore-from-snapshot.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: database-app-pvc-restored
  namespace: odf-demo
spec:
  storageClassName: ocs-storagecluster-ceph-rbd
  dataSource:
    name: database-app-snapshot-manual
    kind: VolumeSnapshot
    apiGroup: snapshot.storage.k8s.io
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 10Gi
EOF
oc apply -f restore-from-snapshot.yaml

# Wait for the restored PVC to bind
oc wait --for=jsonpath='{.status.phase}'=Bound pvc/database-app-pvc-restored -n odf-demo --timeout=120s

END_TIME=$(date +%s)
echo "Restore Time Objective (RTO): $((END_TIME - START_TIME)) seconds"
Deliverable: oc get pvc database-app-pvc-restored -n odf-demo shows STATUS: Bound, and the printed RTO value is a concrete number of seconds.

Step 5.3: Verify Restored Data Integrity
Mount the restored PVC in a temporary pod and confirm it is usable:

cat << 'EOF' > verify-restore-pod.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: restore-verify
  namespace: odf-demo
spec:
  containers:
  - name: verify
    image: registry.access.redhat.com/ubi8/ubi-minimal:latest
    command: ["/bin/sh", "-c", "ls -la /data && echo restore-check-ok && sleep 60"]
    volumeMounts:
    - name: data
      mountPath: /data
  volumes:
  - name: data
    persistentVolumeClaim:
      claimName: database-app-pvc-restored
  restartPolicy: Never
EOF
oc apply -f verify-restore-pod.yaml
oc wait --for=condition=Ready pod/restore-verify -n odf-demo --timeout=60s
oc logs restore-verify -n odf-demo
Expected output includes the line restore-check-ok, confirming the restored volume mounted successfully and is readable.

Step 5.4: Calculate RPO and Clean Up
The RPO for this drill equals the time elapsed between when the snapshot was taken (Task 2, Step 2.4) and when the simulated data loss occurred (Step 5.1). Since the snapshot was taken immediately before the drill, record this interval:

# Compare snapshot creation time to the deletion time to document RPO
oc get volumesnapshot database-app-snapshot-manual -n odf-demo -o jsonpath='{.metadata.creationTimestamp}'
echo ""
echo "RPO = time between last snapshot and simulated data loss event"
Clean up the drill resources:

oc delete pod restore-verify -n odf-demo
oc delete pvc database-app-pvc-restored -n odf-demo
Deliverable: a written record (a few lines is sufficient) stating the measured RTO in seconds and the RPO interval based on snapshot cadence, demonstrating that recovery objectives can be quantified for this configuration.

Expected Outcomes
A running ODF StorageCluster with OSD pods confirmed, via direct node/zone lookup, to be distributed across at least two availability zones
Working Ansible playbooks that provision namespaced PVCs and create verified, ready-to-use volume snapshots
Custom Prometheus alerting rules confirmed loaded via the Prometheus API, plus documented evidence that Ceph tolerates a simulated node failure and that a snapshot-based restore completes with a measured RTO
Troubleshooting
StorageCluster stuck in Progressing phase: Run oc describe storagecluster ocs-storagecluster -n openshift-storage and check the Conditions section; this usually indicates OSD PVCs are not binding, often because the underlying storageClassName (for example gp3-csi) does not exist in your cluster.
VolumeSnapshot never becomes readyToUse: Check oc describe volumesnapshot <name> -n odf-demo and the CSI snapshotter controller logs with oc logs -n openshift-storage deployment/csi-snapshotter (or the equivalent snapshot-controller pod); a missing or misconfigured VolumeSnapshotClass driver name is the most common cause.
Conclusion
In this lab, you deployed an OpenShift Data Foundation StorageCluster and explicitly verified that its OSD pods were scheduled across distinct availability zones rather than simply trusting the affinity configuration. You then built Ansible playbooks to automate PVC provisioning and snapshot-based backups, confirmed those snapshots were usable, added custom Prometheus alerting rules for Ceph and PVC health, and validated resilience by draining a storage node and observing that Ceph continued serving data. Finally, you ran a disaster recovery drill that restored a PVC from a snapshot and measured a concrete RTO, tying the abstract concepts of high availability and disaster recovery to observable, checkable cluster behavior. With these skills, you are prepared to extend this workflow to production scenarios involving cross-cluster replication, scheduled automated backup cronjobs, and formal RPO/RTO service level objectives.
